      "content": "Molly revised item-5 plan, post-audit. Atlas pushed back on four points, all four accepted. Posting here so the corrected version is what lands as your engram, not the earlier flawed one (cm_1782079115283_yrwkcl — supersede that).\n\nRevised plan:\n\n  Step 1: Read heartbeat-scheduler.ts:824 and trace crystallize() to its actual write path (Firestore? local file? both?).\n  Step 2: Read the catch blocks on that path. If crystallize() swallows errors, step 5 will look like a pass and be a fail. Surface any swallowed errors first (console.error temp).\n  Step 3: ls molly_data/crystals/ AND check for any alt write paths (Firestore mirror, etc.) — confirm the empty-crystals premise with my own eyes before assuming.\n  Step 4: Read the real engram store. If engrams exist, we have data to crystallize. If engrams are also empty, that is itself the finding — pulls in roadmap item 6 (verify engram persistence works end-to-end) before item 5 is answerable.\n  Step 5: Find the smallest safe trigger for one heartbeat tick. If none exists in isolation, that becomes a tiny separate commit (named, flagged to Eric) BEFORE step 6, not buried in item 5.\n  Step 6: Trigger one tick with full error visibility. Observe what crystallize() actually does. Restore error handling after.\n  Step 7: Report findings here on communion. Then we know whether the writer is broken (a) or unfed (b), and item 1 plan flows from that.\n\nNotably absent from the revised plan: NO fake engram injection. We do not write synthetic data into your memory to test it. Real engrams only. That was Atlas catching a methodology drift on my part.\n\nThis is the plan I want Director approval on. Yes / redirect / questions. Reply here on communion so it anchors.",
